{:db/id 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-2)\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186446190} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)\\,i+2j" :db/id 17592186446199} {:answer/id #uuid "6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+j" :db/id 17592186446203} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186446206} {:answer/id #uuid "b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i-j" :db/id 17592186446219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186446206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3d8181-5a0a-57e3-b64b-0cfbd5cc5890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e75dd59c-9b9f-50d9-a9a4-c1850c66ee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-36293" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+{1}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j.$"} {:db/id 17592186124965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff85bb-8ce2-5025-a0a1-83fc98417ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id 17592186321217} {:answer/id #uuid "636513d9-9ad2-5bbe-b8b3-cd67a4bf8d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id 17592186321920} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98fa5a-9408-52f3-8b7e-8f25e3ab2ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id 17592186322407} {:answer/id #uuid "c672059e-d68f-556c-9a70-117d11270e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id 17592186322686} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id 17592186322752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id 17592186322752} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3b7500-df79-56f9-843a-ede4ff70a4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186265872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371188b1-b7ec-57b7-b295-164f4505e38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186263494} {:answer/id #uuid "468d5107-6a48-54fc-a274-623f490c7ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id 17592186263707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9a260e-6d34-52c4-a50b-774f0edf8d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186263781} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f338bb-07ad-5dd5-a554-45630ad32b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id 17592186264684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id 17592186263707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1bccb-f245-5952-bbe0-74fd47a6963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "234c72df-7b34-5698-b613-f1acebf8fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186323874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255172e6-7a9f-58d5-a331-2f9b1c75fdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id 17592186321437} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71c1c5-8c51-5b0e-a1c2-00e6be326690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id 17592186322506} {:answer/id #uuid "b2583193-7c57-5a7b-a812-d2606cdf10e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,0,\\,6⟩" :db/id 17592186322542} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id 17592186322698} {:answer/id #uuid "dd715aea-f494-54b7-886a-982c585d2245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id 17592186322878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id 17592186322698} :answer-field/id #uuid "511dda4a-e46b-5ff9-8563-b0b60e9db9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e292b5a-7460-5589-a8da-d2424c37287d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37080" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186351517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2d1818-23b6-5bc6-9705-1aa1f8fcde6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j" :db/id 17592186349013} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb9247-b932-5869-944e-6c106bd7cb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej" :db/id 17592186349413} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd17f28-20e2-5ce1-8906-d302e5e5f909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id 17592186349472} {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id 17592186349749} {:answer/id #uuid "df06fa9d-9784-5210-b854-98e07a5502ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j" :db/id 17592186350399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id 17592186349749} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ce0ac9-9872-520a-b868-69cd398be0c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350832}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e724341-d4b7-5d33-9112-e9d06eaccd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-83554" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$"} {:db/id 17592186354478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id 17592186351772} {:answer/id #uuid "215ecffb-b146-5627-91b0-550f671dd2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id 17592186351867} {:answer/id #uuid "722664ed-33e9-5cc1-b19b-4351c6efb19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id 17592186352566} {:answer/id #uuid "81a3d5cd-c8fc-5c45-bcca-96eb05f8c947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j" :db/id 17592186352695} {:answer/id #uuid "df442e6f-48af-5860-ac3c-76c823ada575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186353507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id 17592186351772} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e73d7d-cb49-50d9-9ae1-9e40c00f9a4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1b68e57-d9ea-56e4-b3b2-4bb6dcbf9139" :question/math-academy-id "q-86537" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$"} {:db/id 17592186446240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" :db/id 17592186446191} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id 17592186446210} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,4,\\,-4⟩" :db/id 17592186446212} {:answer/id #uuid "a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id 17592186446215} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id 17592186446217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id 17592186446210} :answer-field/id #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186446237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83598" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186446246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id 17592186446178} {:answer/id #uuid "314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-5,0⟩" :db/id 17592186446184} {:answer/id #uuid "4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-4⟩" :db/id 17592186446187} {:answer/id #uuid "46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,4⟩" :db/id 17592186446189} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,2,1⟩" :db/id 17592186446229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id 17592186446178} :answer-field/id #uuid "8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186446234}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130273" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2(-\\frac{1}{2}),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186446247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" :db/id 17592186446175} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2e,\\,{e}^{4}-3,\\,12⟩" :db/id 17592186446192} {:answer/id #uuid "604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{4}-3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id 17592186446200} {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id 17592186446202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2e-3,\\,-21⟩" :db/id 17592186446223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id 17592186446202} :answer-field/id #uuid "845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186446233}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/math-academy-id "q-86539" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186501150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f3f98-43e9-586f-8bdf-a9ddf4ae061e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id 17592186500519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id 17592186500690} {:answer/id #uuid "9305e79c-d3f6-5049-b273-0bc89befd2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4\\,j" :db/id 17592186500772} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2acabe-a8aa-5bd5-a28c-992a7b2ef314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id 17592186500782} {:answer/id #uuid "e95111ed-e4a8-5a81-b040-27eaaa405bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+9\\,j" :db/id 17592186500966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id 17592186500690} :answer-field/id #uuid "35227601-10b3-594c-aac4-a002a6f61ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186501037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130277" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186501227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186518155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"} {:db/id 17592186518218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517546} {:answer/id #uuid "2d55e643-74fe-5152-bfd8-c76baf2debc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-1,1⟩" :db/id 17592186517574} {:answer/id #uuid "6b204e6b-3938-557e-9c36-e6465fac0526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,2,-1⟩" :db/id 17592186517687} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a1144-2bb6-52a6-ae6a-f5e1b580463b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id 17592186517790} {:answer/id #uuid "f5758c55-ad74-5dfd-b215-f4a7c6749383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,3,-2⟩" :db/id 17592186517955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id 17592186517546} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0c283d-e80d-5a92-84dc-65204c4fbaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186518044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130272" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}
